## Git workflow

- Branch naming: `feat/<thing>`, `fix/<thing>`, `chore/<thing>`
- Never commit directly to `main`
- Squash on merge; delete branch after
- Never use `--no-verify`, `--force` (without explicit ask), or `reset --hard`
- See the team-wide methodology in [Hornero](https://github.com/<your-org>/hornero) `methodology.md`

## Testing discipline

<Project-specific testing rules. Default starter:>

- Every behavior change ships with a test (RED → GREEN → REFACTOR)
- Bug fixes start with a failing test that reproduces the bug
- E2E tests are reserved for critical-path slices; everything else lives lower in the pyramid
- Flaky tests are fixed, not retried

## Multi-role review

For non-trivial changes, the dispatcher will usually escalate to `/review` automatically. For launches or product-led cross-functional decisions, `/orchestrate` is the better fit — it has the PM frame the work as a customer job, pick the role mix, dispatch in parallel, and synthesise into a decision recommendation.

Slash commands available (definitions in `<path-to-hornero>/.claude/commands/`):

- `/orchestrate <task>` — PM as cross-functional orchestrator (marquee for launches/cross-functional decisions).
- `/review <target>` — direct multi-role-review.
- `/role <name> <task>` — single named role.
- `/kickoff` — new-user walkthrough.
- `/catalog` — what Hornero ships.
- `/extract-lesson` — end-of-session capture.

Available roles in Hornero (all seven ship reviewer subagents in `~/.claude/agents/` when Hornero's `install.sh` has been run):

- `software-engineer` — code, RFCs, ADRs, refactors, TDD
- `product-manager` — PRDs, prioritisation, success metrics, positioning, JTBD
- `designer` — interaction, states, accessibility, platform conventions
- `tester` — risk-driven test plans, exploratory charters, regression tests, pre-release pass
- `security-expert` — threat models, security reviews, LLM-risk, pre-launch security audit
- `marketing-expert` — positioning, narrative, launch briefs (CMO altitude)
- `data-scientist` — metric definitions, experiments, causal claims, pre-launch measurement audit

